  "raw": {
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=b598cb69-0393-4ebc-902c-696995eb5395&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203278351043_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Head Coach - Women's Wrestling",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\n\nLocation: Sioux City\n\nSummary:  The Head Women's Wrestling Coach is responsible for building, leading, and administering all aspects of a competitive NAIA Women's Wrestling program. Grounded in the university's values, mission, and commitment to respect for all persons, the Head Coach oversees student-athlete recruitment, player development, match planning, budget management, academic tracking, and community promotion.   ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES include the following: Other duties may be assigned. Recruitment & Roster Management Actively recruit qualified student-athletes to establish and maintain a full, highly competitive roster, adhering to NAIA eligibility and amateurism guidelines. Evaluate prospective student-athletes at high school, club, and showcase events; build strong relationships with regional high school coaches and athletic directors. Coordinate campus visits, tryouts, and athletic financial aid awards in accordance with university and conference regulations. Program Development & Sport-Specific Leadership Freestyle Wrestling Technical Focus: Updated practice and instructional duties to focus explicitly on international freestyle wrestling rules (the standard for women's collegiate wrestling), including neutral stance, takedowns, leg attacks, par terre defense, turns, exposures, and mat positioning. Weight Management & Athlete Safety: Added explicit oversight for safe, healthy, and compliant weight management, skin checks, and hydration protocols in according with NAIA and NWCA guidelines, collaborating directly with sports medicine staff. Oversee safe, healthy, and compliant athlete weight management, skin checks, and hydration protocols in accordance with NAIA and NWCA rules, collaborating with sports medicine personnel. Analyze match film (e.g., TrackWrestling, FloWrestling) to evaluate individual athlete performance, identify opponent tendencies, and refine personal wrestling plans. Administration, Budgeting & Operations Manage and monitor assigned departmental budget within university guidelines, ensuring fiscal responsibility in travel, equipment, gear, and contest officiating. Schedule intercollegiate non-conference and conference contests in alignment with NAIA, conference, and university policies. Organize all game day logistics, including equipment inventory, mat positioning facility reservations, officials, scorekeepers, and student event staff. Arrange team travel, transportation, lodging, and meal accommodations within university travel procedures. Student-Athlete Experience, Welfare & Academics Foster an inclusive environment of respect, character development, and academic success for all female student-athletes. Monitor academic progress, class attendance, and study hall compliance, collaborating with academic support services to ensure high graduation and retention rates. Promote athlete health, injury prevention, and physical conditioning in coordination with athletic training staff and strength/conditioning coaches. Promotions, Community Engagement & University Service Serve as a primary spokesperson for the Women's Wrestling program, actively promoting the sport and athletic department through media, alumni outreach, and campus events. Regular attendance at Athletic Department staff meetings. Compliance and cooperation with recruiting strategies and goals. Cooperation and engagement with Men’s Wrestling program regarding equipment, facilities, scheduling and camps. Assist with athletic fundraising initiatives, summer camps/clinics, and community engagement projects. Comply fully with all NAIA, conference, and university rules, regulations, and policies; articulate these standards to student-athletes, recruits, parents, and media. Serve on university committees and perform other institutional duties as assigned by the Executive Director of Athletics. EDUCATION and/or EXPERIENCE: Bachelor's Degree: Required; Master's Degree preferred in Sports Administration, Exercise Science, Coaching, Education, or related field. Coaching Experience: Minimum 2–3 years of successful coaching experience at the collegiate, high school varsity, or elite club level NAIA Knowledge: Demonstrated knowledge of NAIA rules, regulations, and recruitment procedures preferred Certifications: First Aid / CPR / AED certification required (or ability to obtain within 30 days of hire); valid driver's license with clear driving record. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES:   Directly supervises all Assistant Women's Wrestling Coaches, graduate assistants, part-time/volunteer coaching staff, and student managers. Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with the institution's policies and applicable state/federal laws. Responsibilities include interviewing, hiring recommendations, training employees, planning and assigning work, appraising performance, and resolving operational concerns.   QUALIFICATION REQUIREMENTS:  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below represent the knowledge, skills, and/or abilities required. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. COMMUNICATION SKILLS: Ability to effectively communicate, instruct, and articulate complex tactical concepts to student-athletes, recruits, parents, media, and institutional leadership. Ability to draft professional email correspondence, scouting briefs, budget reports, and team policy manuals. TECHNICAL & ANALYTICAL SKILLS: Proficiency in video editing and scouting software (e.g., Hudl), spreadsheet applications for budget tracking (e.g., MS Excel), and digital communication tools. Ability to analyze athletic performance data and opponent strategies. LEADERSHIP & INTERPERSONAL ABILITIES: Strong interpersonal skills to mentor student-athletes, resolve interpersonal team conflicts, foster sportsmanship, and collaborate with diverse campus constituents. ORGANIZATIONAL & TACTICAL REASONING: Ability to define tactical problems, synthesize film data, establish game plans, and execute multi-faceted travel and practice schedules under changing conditions. PHYSICAL DEMANDS:  T he physical demands described here represent those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions.   Mat Positioning & Demonstration:  Frequently moves, positions, or traverses across wrestling mats, athletic training facilities, and gymnasium environments during practices and competitions. Demonstration & Instruction:  Regularly positions self to demonstrate or instruct proper wrestling techniques, holds, takedowns, turns and drill setups on wrestling mats. Communication & Alertness:  Frequently communicates with student-athletes, officials, and coaching staff during high-noise practice and tournament environments; must accurately perceive and exchange audible signals, timer cues and whistles. Visual Acuity:  Requires close, distance, peripheral, and depth perception vision to evaluate active wrestling holds, monitor athlete safety on the mat, check skin conditions, and analyze video recordings. Equipment Handling & Lifting:  Frequently moves, transports, or positions wrestling mats, scales, training gear, or athletic equipment weighing up to 25–50 pounds across athletic facility spaces. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Indoor Wrestling Facilities:  Work is primarily conducted indoors in wrestling practice rooms, gymnasiums, and athletic arenas. Environment involves physical contact settings requiring strict adherence to hygiene, skin health, and sanitation protocols. Noise & Activity Levels:  The work environment experiences moderate to high noise levels typical of wrestling practice rooms, dual meets, and multi-mat collegiate tournaments Travel Demands:  Requires evening, weekend, and multi-day overnight travel via university van, bus, or commercial flight for intercollegiate athletic contests and recruiting events.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=b598cb69-0393-4ebc-902c-696995eb5395&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203278349678_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Head Coach - Women's Flag Football",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\n\nLocation: Sioux City\n\nNormal 0 false false false false EN-US X-NONE X-NONE st1\\:*{behavior:url(#ieooui) } /* Style Definitions */ table.MsoNormalTable {mso-style-name:\"Table Normal\"; mso-tstyle-rowband-size:0; mso-tstyle-colband-size:0; mso-style-noshow:yes; mso-style-priority:99; mso-style-parent:\"\"; mso-padding-alt:0in 5.4pt 0in 5.4pt; mso-para-margin:0in; mso-pagination:widow-orphan; font-size:10.0pt; font-family:\"Times New Roman\",serif;} SUMMARY:   The Head Women's Flag Football Coach is responsible for building, leading, and administering all aspects of a competitive NAIA Women's Flag Football program. Grounded in the university's values, mission, and commitment to respect for all persons, the Head Coach oversees student-athlete recruitment, player development, offensive/defensive flag football strategy, game planning, budget management, academic tracking, and community promotion. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES include the following: Other duties may be assigned. Recruitment & Roster Management Actively recruit qualified student-athletes to establish and maintain a full, highly competitive roster, adhering to NAIA eligibility and amateurism guidelines. Evaluate prospective student-athletes at high school, club, and showcase flag football events; build strong relationships with regional high school coaches and athletic directors. Coordinate campus visits, tryouts, and athletic financial aid awards in accordance with university and conference regulations. Program Development & Sport-Specific Leadership Design, execute, and evaluate comprehensive practice plans, tactical offensive/defensive schemes, flag-pulling techniques, route running, quarterback mechanics, and game strategies specific to 7v7 / NAIA Women's Flag Football rules. Instruct student-athletes on sport-specific skills, rule nuances (e.g., line of scrimmage restrictions, rusher standards, downfield blocking/screening, and flag belt protocol), and team concepts during practices and competitions. Analyze game film (e.g., Hudl) to evaluate team performance, prepare competitive scouting reports, and formulate game strategies. Administration, Budgeting & Operations Manage and monitor assigned departmental budget within university guidelines, ensuring fiscal responsibility in travel, equipment, gear, and contest officiating. Schedule intercollegiate non-conference and conference contests in alignment with NAIA, conference, and university policies. Organize all game day logistics, including equipment inventory, field setup, facility reservations, officials, scorekeepers, and student event staff. Arrange team travel, transportation, lodging, and meal accommodations within university travel procedures. Recruit, Manage and Develop coaching staff, graduate assistants and volunteers. Student-Athlete Experience, Welfare & Academics Foster an inclusive environment of respect, character development, and academic success for all female student-athletes. Monitor academic progress, class attendance, and study hall compliance, collaborating with academic support services to ensure high graduation and retention rates. Promote athlete health, injury prevention, and physical conditioning in coordination with athletic training staff and strength/conditioning coaches. Promotions, Community Engagement & University Service Serve as a primary spokesperson for the Women's Flag Football program, actively promoting the sport and athletic department through media, alumni outreach, and campus events. Regular attendance at Athletic Department staff meetings. Compliance and cooperation with recruiting strategies and goals. Cooperation and engagement with Men’s Football program regarding shared equipment, facilities, scheduling and camps. Assist with athletic fundraising initiatives, summer flag football camps/clinics, and community engagement projects. Comply fully with all NAIA, conference, and university rules, regulations, and policies; articulate these standards to student-athletes, recruits, parents, and media. Serve on university committees and perform other institutional duties as assigned by the Executive Director of Athletics. EDUCATION and/or EXPERIENCE: Bachelor's Degree: Required; Master's Degree preferred in Sports Administration, Exercise Science, Coaching, Education, or related field. Coaching Experience: Minimum 2–3 years of successful coaching experience at the collegiate, high school varsity, or elite club level (Flag Football, Tackle Football, or related team sports highly preferred) NAIA Knowledge: Demonstrated knowledge of NAIA rules, regulations, and recruitment procedures preferred Certifications: First Aid / CPR / AED certification required (or ability to obtain within 30 days of hire); valid driver's license with clear driving record. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES:   Directly supervises all Assistant Women's Flag Football Coaches, part-time/volunteer coaching staff, and student managers. Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with the institution's policies and applicable state/federal laws. Responsibilities include interviewing, hiring recommendations, training employees, planning and assigning work, appraising performance, and resolving operational concerns.   QUALIFICATION REQUIREMENTS:   To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below represent the knowledge, skills, and/or abilities required. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. COMMUNICATION SKILLS: Ability to effectively communicate, instruct, and articulate complex tactical concepts to student-athletes, recruits, parents, media, and institutional leadership. Ability to draft professional email correspondence, scouting briefs, budget reports, and team policy manuals.   TECHNICAL & ANALYTICAL SKILLS: Proficiency in video editing and scouting software (e.g., Hudl), spreadsheet applications for budget tracking (e.g., MS Excel), and digital communication tools. Ability to analyze athletic performance data and opponent strategies.   LEADERSHIP & INTERPERSONAL ABILITIES: Strong interpersonal skills to mentor student-athletes, resolve interpersonal team conflicts, foster sportsmanship, and collaborate with diverse campus constituents.   ORGANIZATIONAL & TACTICAL REASONING: Ability to define tactical problems, synthesize film data, establish game plans, and execute multi-faceted travel and practice schedules under changing conditions.   PHYSICAL DEMANDS:   The physical demands described here represent those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Movement & Field Positioning:  Frequently moves, positions, or traverses across outdoor athletic fields, indoor turf facilities, and gymnasium courts during practices and games. Demonstration & Instruction:  Regularly positions self to demonstrate or instruct proper flag football mechanics, flag pulling, route running, and drill setups. Communication & Alertness:  Frequently communicates with student-athletes, officials, and coaching staff during high-noise practice and game environments; must accurately perceive and exchange audible signals, play calls, and whistles. Visual Acuity:  Requires close, distance, peripheral, and depth perception vision to evaluate active game play, monitor athlete safety across large field spaces, and analyze video recordings. Equipment Handling & Lifting:  Frequently moves, transports, or positions sports equipment, flags, cones, footballs, agility gear, or training aids weighing up to 25–50 pounds across athletic facilities. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Outdoor & Weather Exposure:  Work is conducted both indoors and in outdoor athletic field settings. Position involves regular exposure to variable outdoor weather conditions, including sun, heat, cold, wind, and precipitation. Noise & Activity Levels:  The work environment experiences moderate to high noise levels typical of athletic practices, athletic facilities, and live competitive games. Travel Demands:  Requires evening, weekend, and multi-day overnight travel via university van, bus, or commercial flight for intercollegiate athletic contests and recruiting events.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=b598cb69-0393-4ebc-902c-696995eb5395&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203251581553_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\n\nLocation: Sioux City\n\nJob Description: We are looking for an organized, eager, and engaging Admissions Counselor. This is a twelve-month position with primary objective for recruiting targeted students to Briar Cliff University in Sioux City, Iowa. The counselor will assist in developing and implementing appropriate strategies to meet individual and organized recruiting goals. This includes working with prospective students and their families about their college choice, promoting the University, and assisting in all related campus events. Job Duties:  Recruit new students Manage a recruiting territory Some recruiting travel along with evening and weekend work may be required High schools Fairs Community College Special Events Coordinate inquiries and initiate the appropriate follow up for each student Work with student from beginning to end of their enrollment process Assist in answering any questions that the student may have Conduct campus and virtual visits Connect with faculty and staff that may be able to assist Initiates follow up and communication with student via call, text, and email Assist with special recruiting projects Work closely with support staff Participate in Enrollment Management meetings Complete weekly and monthly progress reports Represent the admissions office in a professional manner at and off campus events Other duties as assigned Education and Experience:  Bachelor’s degree from a four-year college or university One or two years related experience or training Sales experience preferred Qualifications:  Computer Knowledge Efficient in Word and Excel Basic typing and calculator skills Ability to adhere to communicate effectively on a variety of platforms Time management and organizational skills Self-motivated with a positive attitude Ability to work under pressure in a fast-paced environment Ability to work and live within a Catholic Franciscan culture of acceptance and service to all",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=b598cb69-0393-4ebc-902c-696995eb5395&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203228004466_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Ground Technicians",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\n\nLocation: Sioux City\n\nPOSITION SUMMARY: Under general supervision, performs a variety of general maintenance duties which include electrical, mechanical, carpentry, and construction in the maintenance and repair of college facilities and equipment. EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, AND LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS: (A comparable amount of training, education or experience may be substituted for the minimum qualifications.) · Completion of high school/GED · Three years related maintenance work experience ESSENTIAL TASKS: (Employee must be able to perform the following functions to the satisfaction of the employee’s supervisor.) · Inspect buildings, electrical systems, grounds, and equipment to ensure safe, well-maintained conditions, identify hazards, defects, and the need for adjustment or repair. · Perform minor troubleshooting and repairs; replace light bulbs, ballasts, and fuses. · Assist with preventive maintenance and troubleshooting on HVAC system, changing filters, bearings. · Complete maintenance work orders as assigned and in a timely manner. · Identify and perform basic service and repair on plumbing fixtures · Identify and assist with carpentry and repair work. · Operate a variety of machinery, equipment and tools including saws, router, drills, sanders, planers, drill presses and various hand tools. · Maintain inventory of tools, supplies, and equipment; recommend tools, supplies, and equipment for purchase. · Perform a variety of locksmith duties; install, repair, and replace locks on doors · Assist with snow removal · Perform periodic maintenance of lawn equipment and vehicles. · Assist with moving loading, unloading, and storing supplies, furniture, and equipment. · Respond to 24-hour emergency calls · Adjustment of hours and/or weekend work may be required and/or occasional overtime. · Perform assigned responsibilities, duties, and tasks according to established practices, procedures, techniques, and standards in a safe manner and with minimal supervision. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: · Thorough knowledge of proper safety techniques and procedures while using chemicals, power tools, hand tools and equipment · Knowledge of proper lifting techniques · Ability to operate heavy equipment · Understanding of methods, techniques, materials, tools, and equipment used in maintenance, repair, and electrical work · Ability to properly perform basic welding · Ability to use required tools and equipment skillfully and safely, understanding of building codes. COMMUNICATION SKILLS: Ability to establish and maintain effective and cooperative working relationships with employees. Ability to interact and communicate effectively in a tactful and courteous manner with a variety of people, including students, employees, faculty and the general public. Ability to manage interpersonal conflict situations requiring tact, diplomacy and discretion; ability to verbalize understanding of a problem or situation, ask relevant questions for clarification, and explain it to others. Follow instructions or work orders; and/or read routine sentences, instructions, regulations, or procedures; complete routine job forms and incident reports; and/or communicate routine information regarding daily activities. DECISION-MAKING AND ANALYTICAL SKILLS: Requires limited decision making; perform manual duties; operate in a safe and efficient manner. Ability to understand and follow written and oral directions; ability to complete assignments without immediate supervision; ability to perform all essential functions using safe work methods and following safety regulations relating to job. EQUIPMENT/SOFTWARE: use computers for data entry and/or handle, use, or repair hand-held power equipment, tools and/or light machinery. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: (The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential tasks of this job.) Regularly required to stand, walk, use hands and fingers to handle or feel objects, tools, or controls; and talk or hear. Occasionally required to sit. Frequently must squat, stoop, or kneel, reach above the head and reach forward. Daily uses hand strength to grasp tools, supplies and equipment. Occasionally climbs ladders. Frequently lift and/or move up to 25 pounds and occasionally lift and/or move up to 100 pounds. WORK ENVIRONMENT: (The work environment described here is representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job.) Regularly works indoors and outdoors. Occasionally work with toxic or caustic chemicals such as petroleum products, degreasers, and sprays. Frequently exposed to risk of electrical shock, mechanical hazards, explosive hazards, burn hazards, chemical hazards. Meet deadlines with severe time constraints. Moderate noise level. Frequently exposed to wet and/or humid conditions; fumes or airborne particles; outside weather conditions; extreme cold; extreme heat.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=b598cb69-0393-4ebc-902c-696995eb5395&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9203178044339_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Radiologic Technology Program Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\n\nLocation: Sioux City\n\nThe Department of Nursing at Briar Cliff University seeks to fill a position as Director of the Radiologic Technology Program and Assistant or Associate Professor of Radiologic Technology. This position is to begin in Spring 2027 (preferred) or Summer 2027. This is a 12-month, tenure-eligible position focused on advancing knowledge through teaching, professional development, and service within the university community.  The successful candidate will direct the University’s program in radiologic technology, including assisting with recruiting, identifying clinical sites, advising students, and providing strategic direction for the program. They will also teach courses in radiologic technology as appropriate to the candidate’s area of expertise. The program director should be very familiar with the requirements of JRCERT disciplinary accreditation, as they will lead the faculty in the maintenance of program accreditation. Additionally, faculty engage in scholarship and professional development, complete institutional and departmental service, participate in departmental and university assessment, and advise students in experiential learning opportunities in addition to their coursework, supporting students’ professional growth and contributing to the broader educational mission.  The successful candidate should have a minimum of a Master of Education or Master of Science in radiologic science, health science education, or allied health leadership; a terminal degree is preferred. They should have current ARRT certification and registration and prior clinical experience. Candidates in the process of pursuing a terminal degree will be considered, and these ABD candidates must have their terminal degrees completed no later than the end of their second year in the position.  The successful candidate should demonstrate excellence in teaching at the university level, a strong record of successfully advising and mentoring students, an interest in maintaining their own intellectual creativity, and an ability to contribute to curriculum development and academic service.  Founded in 1930 and rooted in the Catholic Franciscan tradition, Briar Cliff University is a vibrant, values-based community dedicated to academic excellence and personal growth. Located on the scenic Loess Hills overlooking the Missouri River, \"The Cliff\" maintains a tight-knit learning environment with an institutional 13:1 student-to-faculty ratio. Academically, the university offers over 30 undergraduate programs and several respected graduate degrees, including regionally recognized programs in Nursing, Physical Therapy, and Sport Management. Our commitment to the student-athlete experience is equally robust; as members of the NAIA and Great Plains Athletic Conference, the Briar Cliff Chargers have earned 19 conference championships and were recently honored as an NAIA Champions of Character Gold Level Institution. A city of 85,000 stretching across the corners of Iowa, South Dakota, and Nebraska (referred to as “Siouxland”), Sioux City is nestled along the Sioux and Missouri Rivers approximately 80 miles from both Omaha, NE, and Sioux Falls, SD. The city offers a vibrant, friendly community with a cost of living that is attractive for families and individuals alike. Sioux City possesses a bustling riverfront, a lively downtown arts and entertainment scene, numerous parks, and rich cultural opportunities, combining small-town charm with the amenities of a larger metropolitan area. As a regional hub for business and culture, the city features over 50 parks, the historic Orpheum Theatre, Tyson Events Center, and a mix of national retailers and award-winning local favorites. Sioux City has twice been named an “All-America City,” highlighting its strong community and significant quality of life.  Candidates should submit:  A letter of interest describing the alignment of the candidate's qualifications with the position requirements Current curriculum vita A statement of teaching philosophy that addresses ways in which the candidate creates and sustains a culture of belonging, fairness and appreciation for different perspectives in the classroom Graduate and undergraduate transcripts (unofficial copies are fine at this point) Contact information (only) for three professional references For full consideration, candidates should submit their applications no later than September 30, 2026, although review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Any final offer is contingent on a successful background check. If you have any questions about this position, contact Provost@briarcliff.edu . A competitive salary is commensurate with teaching experience and academic credentials. This position is subject to final budget approval.",
      "department": ""
    },
